WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.840
In the previous videos, you could see us doing many projects. 

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.640
Setting up Basekamp with containers, decks and roofs.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.960
Renovating a ruin, or transforming the landscape.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.240
But, we rarely talk about other stuff at the Kamp, 
that is essential for our project.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.760
In this video, we will show them to you.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.760
Welcome to a new Project Kamp update.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.200
So, the Project Kamp you have normally seen in 
the videos, has a lot to do with building...

00:00:29.454 --> 00:00:30.420
Renovating...

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.524
Digging...

00:00:34.456 --> 00:00:35.567
And, chopping...

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.200
But, there is a lot of things
that happens behind the scenes.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.160
And, many of you asked in the comments
what happens beyond the projects we do.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.440
And, in this video, we will show you.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:47.840
Come with me!

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.160
So, the first thing you need to know
is that we are organize in seasons.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.403
From April to November, we work on projects,

00:00:54.403 --> 00:00:58.960
During the winter, we reflect and plan,
on what will come next season.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.680
We have people staying long term,
and managing the project over the different seasons.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.520
And then, we have short term people coming to help,
 with their skills for a part or for the whole season.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.960
But, as we grow, we need systems to organize.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.280
One of the important ones is the weekly planning board.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.480
This is our weekly plan board!

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:27.054
Every monday morning, we meet here with the whole group,
 and we organize our week.

00:01:27.054 --> 00:01:30.680
We mark events here that will happen during the day.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.560
People that will be coming, people that will be going...

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:42.120
We also have the teams that clean 
throughout the week on each specific day.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.960
And then, we also use this time 
to try to learn a bit of Portuguese every week.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.640
We have a team that prepares the bread.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.200
And, a team responsible 
to prepare the pizza night, on Friday night.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.920
And then, the development work in the Kamp
is organized around different teams

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.360
Like, the workspace, which renovates,
builds and creates awesome stuff!

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:14.960
Or, general help that clears out invasive species, plants native trees,
or just discovers waterfalls and lagoons.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.040
So, these are the teams that you normally see,
when the projects are being made.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.000
But, in order for these projects
to be uploaded on YouTube, we need a--

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.160
At the moment, there are two people making the videos:
Javier and Gui.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.200
And, also, Dave is always around helping.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.320
Now, I realize you don't know much about Gui, 
so maybe it's a good moment to introduce him.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.920
Hi, my name is Guilherme!

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.920
I'm 20 years old, and I am from Portugal.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.760
Here, I am a video editor.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:58.581
But, I am also passionate about nature, music, coding, 
bikes, hand work, meeting people, and apparently fixing stuff that I break...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.442
I came to Project Kamp,
because I wanted to work so much with Javier.

00:03:05.471 --> 00:03:08.471
I came to Project Kamp,
because I was really inspired by it.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.400
And, I believe that the project
is doing something good for the planet.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.800
I don't have a concrete thing that I want to do in the future...

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:21.240
But, for sure, I want to do something,
that makes the world around me a little bit better...

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.129
Just like this video!

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:23.720
So, keep watching it!

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.600
Now that you know the video making team, 
they can explain as our Project Kamp videos are created.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.504
Question number one: how do we do the videos?

00:03:32.504 --> 00:03:38.560
Last season, we set a system where people will actually
record themselves, like the team that are doing the projects.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.600
Then, they download all the clips
in a computer we have over there.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.360
So, here's an old MacBook
with a broken screen.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.280
We took it off and now we have a screen.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.160
So, we have this funky, weird laptop...

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.800
Well, it's not a laptop, but a PC...

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:52.617
I don't know...

00:03:52.617 --> 00:03:53.641
This thing.

00:03:54.524 --> 00:03:57.531
With that footage, we will go in and start editing the video.

00:03:57.531 --> 00:04:01.737
We brought all the files, put it in a timeline, start chopping...

00:04:01.737 --> 00:04:02.840
- The chopping!

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.720
♩ And I love chopping ♩

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.215
And then, yeah, we figured out what's missing.

00:04:07.215 --> 00:04:10.939
The script, some voiceovers and intros,
 record what's needed,

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:15.360
And then, put of all the pieces together with some nice music,
and some background noises.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.480
And, that's pretty much it.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:19.531
- Sounds easy!

00:04:19.531 --> 00:04:20.737
It's not.

00:04:22.076 --> 00:04:27.280
We have three Sony ZV-1's, six GoPro's...

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:30.840
- So, one GoPro flashed away.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.560
Now, it's somewhere there.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.240
But, there are a lot of brambles...

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:40.040
We like to buy secondhand,
and these ones are cheaper and still good quality.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:46.475
- If you have anything to replace, or just for 
the sake of teaching the people how to use the cameras...

00:04:46.475 --> 00:04:48.360
It's better if it's just the same model.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.200
- We also have a drone: a MoVI Pro, that I just broke.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.320
And then, repaired, so that's good...

00:04:57.594 --> 00:04:59.542
For music, we use SoundStripe.

00:04:59.542 --> 00:05:03.177
It's a paid platform
where you can get music

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.480
- Is that where you found the banjo music?

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.080
And, this funky music.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.360
- I like funky music.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:12.660
I like it, but...

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:15.295
People prefer folk sometimes

00:05:15.295 --> 00:05:17.640
It's hard to be good to everyone.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.360
- It's hard for him not to copy my music.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.154
Yeah...

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.720
We use DaVinci Resolve for editing.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:32.640
We used to use Premiere, but it's paid to compatibility issues.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.923
So, we stick with DaVinci.

00:05:34.923 --> 00:05:37.323
Its free! It's good! It does the job!

00:05:37.323 --> 00:05:38.600
- Sorry Adobe.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:39.800
I think they can manage...

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.545
- And, how are the flies?

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:44.772
They're everywhere!

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.760
Sticking to my sweating skin.

00:05:49.132 --> 00:05:50.800
I also have a question for you, Dave!

00:05:51.266 --> 00:05:52.378
- What's that?

00:05:52.378 --> 00:05:53.800
Can I get the camera?

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:54.960
Okay! 

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.040
So Dave, how about the results of the channel and the videos?

00:06:00.948 --> 00:06:01.786
It's cool!

00:06:01.786 --> 00:06:04.800
I think the main challenge for us is always to integrate it properly.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:11.242
The people in the film know how to do it,
and then deliver good files to the editor.

00:06:11.242 --> 00:06:12.360
So, the system feels pretty good.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.440
And also, the channel, I mean, 
everyone leaves good comments!

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:16.960
Channel is growing, so it's cool.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.640
Yeah, this year, we started really going more
for integrating Patreons, which has been really cool.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:27.889
Also, to not depend too much on Google,
to pay the bills but more direct from people. 

00:06:27.889 --> 00:06:30.960
Which, yeah, it's been growing a lot, 
so that's really cool!

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.080
Feels like the long term thing, 
that's going to fuel up Project Kamp.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.800
So, one thing we're gonna try...

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.813
I know, it's a sensitive point.

00:06:42.813 --> 00:06:44.280
It's to integrate an advertisement.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:52.110
Mainly because it will give us quite an extra amount of money,
to just build buildings and stuff.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:57.480
And we already have this anyway from YouTube, 
but we also don't want to compromise the video.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:01.920
So, we're trying to find this balance,
and selecting things that we actually think make sense.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.320
So, being very critical in the product...

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:07.560
And also, finding a way to integrate it
nicely in the video.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.040
So, we're going to give that a try!

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.360
We're not sure if we're going to keep it.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.640
So, if y'all don't like it, we will stop it.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:15.600
But, we want to at least give it a try...

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:16.960
So, we get real YouTubers!

00:07:19.323 --> 00:07:26.435
With that, we're going to make it that the Patreons
don't see any advertisements, if you support on Patreon.

00:07:26.901 --> 00:07:29.746
And, we also are going to start
uploading in 4K.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.760
So, even more reasons to become a Patreon!

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:38.760
We're trying to find this balance,
between how to sustain a project, and get enough income...

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.880
But, yeah, do it in a way that you think makes sense.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:48.664
So, we're exploring sturdy options,
whether it's Patreon, YouTube and other kind of funding.

00:07:48.664 --> 00:07:51.600
Like, we don't know yet,
so we're still figuring it out...

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.560
So, money comes in, but where does it go?

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.040
Well, a big part goes into developing the projects itself.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.720
For instance:  buying materials, tools, containers, a trailer...

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:10.440
But, then, in order to have a lot of people in the Kamp 
to make this projects happen, we need to take care of them.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:14.960
So, being with 20 people here, it's around €5 per person per day.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.800
That means €3,000 on supplying people's needs at the Kamp.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:24.920
And, out of that, the biggest part goes into food,
for which our kitchen team is responsible.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:26.645
Meet Timo and Paule!

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:33.600
So, we're two people that are working in the kitchen.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:37.960
And, we're cooking two meals per day, during the week.

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.360
It's important to the people to receive their meals on time.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:48.126
So, we try to be finished at 1 PM with lunch,
 and 7 PM with a dinner.

00:08:48.126 --> 00:08:51.320
And, also, it helps to have two people in the kitchen, I guess!

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:57.320
Because, when one is feeling more hectic, 
you can kind of balance it out a bit.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.600
It's nice to have a little bit more time, to divide the tasks.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.600
And yeah, don't be stressed in the kitchen at all!

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:08.632
We have a super big fridge,
 that is sometimes very full, and a bit chaotic...

00:09:08.632 --> 00:09:11.568
But, it helps us store a lot of food!

00:09:11.568 --> 00:09:17.101
We have a lot of variety in the meals
we've prepared, in terms of veggies.

00:09:17.101 --> 00:09:23.871
To me, the kitchen when I came here
already offered a lot of different spices I could use.

00:09:24.165 --> 00:09:26.280
I like the pans we're cooking with!

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:34.379
It's a lot of fun, a lot of creativity needed, 
and I really like it to have a fun.

00:09:34.379 --> 00:09:38.040
Especially, cooking for 20 people!

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:43.280
Sometimes, it's hard and a challenge
to do for the right amount.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:49.820
Also, what I really like is to really reuse the leftovers,
 and making new stuff out of it.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.960
Or, just putting it in the salad, or stuff like this.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.520
That's one of the things that makes the cooking
most interesting to me here!

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:59.925
Because, we have a set of veggies, that are available to us

00:09:59.925 --> 00:10:06.703
And, we find ways of creating interesting meals with them,
that are also like balance in terms of the diets.

00:10:06.743 --> 00:10:08.880
For me, it's not a lot of planning at all.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.880
You can improvise, and you can plan, of course...

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.120
We have a board to plan the whole week actually, 
but I have never used it...

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:24.080
Besides what Paule said about planning, 
which is more like the 'day-to-day-prepare-every-meal' stuff...

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.920
We do have to take care of having enough supplies here.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:33.320
We go shopping twice a week to four different places.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:37.680
There's a small farm run by Nuno,
who grows into regenerative farming.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:41.080
There's a bigger farm growing in an organic way.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.840
The other two suppliers are in two shops.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.840
One is a bio-shop run by a Dutch couple.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:55.320
But, we're trying to move more towards a small bulk shop,
where we get most of our bulk supplies nowadays.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:00.484
We basically try to document everything that we buy and consume,

00:11:00.484 --> 00:11:03.880
Knowing our baseline consumption, is it organic?

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:06.160
Is it grown in a regenerative way?

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.320
We find out which are the goods, that have the most impact.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:20.040
Maybe on climate, or social stuff that we can then 
switch for other goods.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:23.040
To have a better, or a less of an impact on the environment.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.868
As they said, we source our food
from outside the Kamp,

00:11:26.868 --> 00:11:30.532
But, the bread, that is something we prefer to bake on our own,

00:11:30.532 --> 00:11:34.400
Since, we have a nice clay oven,
 and a lot of mimosas for fuel.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.814
Right now, I'm at the last stage of the bread making.

00:11:43.814 --> 00:11:45.249
The way we make bread.

00:11:45.249 --> 00:11:52.840
I'm oiling the molds, and I'm also going to knead the bread
for the last time and cut it in pieces.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:59.760
We started by one, but right now we are more people,
so we are baking twice a week.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.760
Six breads times two, so twelve breads a week.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:07.800
Which, in total is like 13 or 14 kilos of bread!

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:11.640
Right now, we are doing 50/50.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:15.000
50% of the white flour, and 50% whole grain.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:19.560
We have a bread team, and we are with four people.

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:22.248
Two of them are more into it.

00:12:23.596 --> 00:12:28.600
Some of them are starting to learn
how to bake and how to make bread.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:32.840
I started learning from Adrian and from Javier.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:35.240
Yeah, I just got into it.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:36.534
I liked it!

00:12:36.534 --> 00:12:39.560
By the week, it was getting better and better.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:46.142
So, we started trying new things and different ways  
of making the dough and baking.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:51.720
I think, right now, we have pretty good bread,
and I'm happy with it!

00:12:54.382 --> 00:13:02.520
Also, because I like the way people react,
to new bread every week and that it's getting better.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:11.040
I would say the most tricky part is the oven temperature,
 and how to bake it, or the time you bake it.

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:15.560
So, if you want to learn,
you can take the research module!

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:35.240
Apart from the video making andfood, 
we have a very important area behind the scenes.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:40.811
A big difference with other off grid projects
 is that we document much of the work we do.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:44.200
We share it online and open source.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:49.680
This is part of our mission, and the first step for that,
is having a proper documentation to share.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:54.400
So, the documenting is about getting
all the technical information.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:14:02.886
From all the projects, the materials, the cost of the materials,
 the tools we've used for building the project...

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:08.680
And also, it's putting the measurements
 in 3D-models and 2D-drawings.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:12.040
So, it's easy for people to replicate it.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:16.680
Like, to replicate the whole project,
or just replicate that little part of it.

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:21.007
Right now, we are documenting the toilet.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:26.043
We will be able to upload it in some weeks.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:29.880
And, here you can see the door! A nice door!

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:37.120
We've already done the tent,
and we've already documented the roof from the container.

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:40.800
We have seen ourselves like ready to start with it!

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:46.400
So, that we can little by little upload all the projects we have.

00:14:49.676 --> 00:14:52.955
We can fulfill the vision of the project

00:14:52.955 --> 00:15:00.360
Basically, it's giving all this information we have,
 in an open source.

00:15:00.759 --> 00:15:04.747
Okay, at this point, maybe it's worth to explain some stuff first.

00:15:04.747 --> 00:15:07.960
Project Kamp belongs to a bigger family, called One Army.

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:14.760
Which developed other projects, like Precious Plastic,
that shares tools and machines to recycle plastic.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:18.240
To create a community of plastic recyclers around the world.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:22.880
Or, Fixing Fashion, that want people to help
to repair and upgrade their clothes.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:25.119
So, there is less textile waste.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:31.320
All these projects work on big global problems and are open source,
which means everyone can join.

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.200
Have a look on onearmy.earth to see them!

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:40.899
Anyway, for this wealso started to develop 
our own software to build communities.

00:15:40.899 --> 00:15:43.560
So, people can share knowledge, and collaborate with each other.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.920
We call this software Community Platform.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.240
Sean and Dave can tell us more about it.

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:53.160
So, the community platform underpins
all of the One Army projects.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.467
So, Fixing Fashion, Precious Plastic, Project Kamp...

00:15:56.467 --> 00:15:58.119
There's different modules in that.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:03.994
How-to's, research, the map, events, 
and different projects using different modules.

00:16:03.994 --> 00:16:08.076
There's lots of contributors and there's a handful of maintainers

00:16:08.395 --> 00:16:12.759
Most of them are spread across the world,
 and work remotely when they can.

00:16:13.426 --> 00:16:17.612
I'm sort trying out working here on the site of Project Kamp,
which is going well, I think.

00:16:18.151 --> 00:16:19.240
I'm enjoying it anyway!

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:24.040
- We used to have forums and stuff, 
which is a way for peopleto talk with each other on the internet.

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:30.085
But, it became a bit messy, because some people start talking
 a lot into a sort of a quick conversation...

00:16:30.085 --> 00:16:34.080
And then, you have the good knowledge,
 which was hidden and you couldn't find that...

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.920
Some features were missing, 
like a map to show the entire community.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:41.528
So, we started developing our own community software.

00:16:41.528 --> 00:16:46.920
Basically, it's software to build these communities,
 because we believe they should exist more around the world.

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:53.000
And, yeah, this way we can decide for ourselves,
of what features it should have.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:57.400
And, also reward for instance, 
users that make very good long term information.

00:16:58.640 --> 00:17:04.640
So you can put the incentives at the right place, 
which is currently not necessarily the case in other platforms.

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:06.623
Yeah, so, it's one of those small little things

00:17:06.623 --> 00:17:12.400
Instead of liking popular content, we have a useful-button
 sp you say this thing is useful!

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:16.560
And, there's a lot of aim for that,
like trying to make useful things.

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:21.400
So, one of the things we've noticed is that 
it's quite complex to build software.

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:26.280
Many little hooks and things and interactions,
and so it's quite a big ambitious project.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.480
Even though it's all online,
it's a big piece of infrastructure.

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:33.200
So, the cool thing is it's all online,
and the code is there.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:37.680
Open source, you could get replicated,
use it, or help out to improve it.

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:43.320
So, if you're a coder and you want to help out,
then I would say go to our GitHub or Discord!

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:46.269
And, you can help building the software!

00:17:46.269 --> 00:17:47.160
- Link in the description!

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:48.200
Yeah! Yes!

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:55.480
By now, you know how our week goes,
but Friday's a bit different.

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:59.160
Because, it's the time we take care
 to maintain and to clean Basekamp.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:04.040
We do it all together in the afternoon,
so we don't get distracted during the week.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.240
And, the tasks seems much easier
when we do it collectively.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:11.280
We clean the kitchen, the workspace, 
the toilet and the shower.

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:15.120
We also repair stuff,
do some brush cutting and weeding.

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:18.800
Once that's is done, then we have what we call human hour.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:22.200
This is a time when we take care of ourselves as a group.

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:26.760
And, we share how we are doing, how we feel,
and give some feedback to each other.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:31.280
It's kind of a special moment,
which help us to build a sense of community.

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:37.480
After all of this, we can start the weekend together,
and we come together.

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:40.760
We light up the oven and we normally eat pizza.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.400
Although we are trying to introduce
other dishes for some more variety.

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:45.840
And, that's all for now!

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:47.901
I will leave you for this week.

